{
  "MCAT Test__e2a0feee0e091fb9": {
    "Question #: 1": {
      "Title": "Exam MCAT Test topic 1 question 495 discussion - ExamTopics",
      "Question": {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Several techniques have been developed to determine the order of a reaction. The rate of a reaction cannot be predicted on the basis of the overall equation, but can be predicted on the basis of the rate-determining step. For instance, the following reaction can be broken down into three steps."
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "A + D \u05d2\u2020\u2019 F + G -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Step 1 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "A \u05d2\u2020\u2019 B + C -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "(slow)"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Step 2 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "B + D \u05d2\u2020\u2019 E + F -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "(fast)"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Step 3 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "E + C \u05d2\u2020\u2019 G -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "(fast)"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Reaction 1 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "In this case, the first step in the reaction pathway is the rate-determining step. Therefore, the overall rate of the reaction must equal the rate of the first step, k"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "[A]"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "where k"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "is the rate constant for the first step. (Rate constants of the different steps are denoted by kx, where x is the step number.)"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "In some cases, it is desirable to measure the rate of a reaction in relation to only one species. In a second-order reaction, for instance, a large excess of one species is included in the reaction vessel. Since a relatively small amount of this large concentration is reacted, we assume that the concentration essentially remains unchanged. Such a reaction is called a pseudo first-order reaction. A new rate constant, k', is established, equal to the product of the rate constant of the original reaction, k, and the concentration of the species in excess. This approach is often used to analyze enzyme activity."
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "In some cases, the reaction rate may be dependent on the concentration of a short-lived intermediate. This can happen if the rate-determining step is not the first step. In this case, the concentration of the intermediate must be derived from the equilibrium constant of the preceding step. For redox reactions, the equilibrium can be correlated with the voltage produced by two half-cells by means of the Nernst equation. This equation states that at any given moment:"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "https://www.examtopics.com/assets/media/exam-media/03828/0066300001.png"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Equation 1 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "When -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "a A + b B \u05d2\u2020\u2019 c C + d D"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Reaction 2 -"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Note: R = 8.314 J/K\u05b2\u00b7mol; F = 9.6485 \u05b3\u2014 10"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "C/mol.)"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "What is the effect of increasing the concentration of reactants in a voltaic cell?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "Choices": {
        "A": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A. The voltage increases, while the spontaneity of the reaction remains the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        "B": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "B. The spontaneity of the reaction increases, but the voltage remains the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        "C": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "C. Both the voltage and the spontaneity of the reaction increase."
            }
          ]
        },
        "D": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "D. The reaction rate increases, but the voltage and spontaneity of the reaction are unchanged."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "Answer": {
        "content": []
      },
      "Explanation": {
        "content": []
      },
      "Vote_count": [
        {
          "voted_answers": "A",
          "vote_count": 1,
          "is_most_voted": true
        }
      ]
    },
